1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,042
Somewhere in a chemical plant,
a radar level transmitter

2
00:00:04,042 --> 00:00:07,299
watches the surface of a
liquid, twenty meters below.

3
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:11,563
It knows the full echo profile,
its own electronics temperature,

4
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:14,567
the condition of its antenna,
and weeks of trends.

5
00:00:14,917 --> 00:00:16,559
And it reports — one number.

6
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:20,286
A current between four and
twenty milliamps, on two wires.

7
00:00:20,706 --> 00:00:25,705
If you're lucky, a trickle of digital
data rides along at 1,200 bits per second

8
00:00:25,705 --> 00:00:28,237
— the speed of an early telephone modem.

9
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,901
Meanwhile, a few hundred meters away,

10
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:35,939
the office network moves a billion bits
per second — almost a million times more.

11
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,831
This video is about the
technology that finally brings

12
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:44,348
Ethernet all the way out to
the instrument: Ethernet-APL.

13
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,283
We'll take it apart all the way down:
how a bit becomes a voltage,

14
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:52,520
how that voltage travels
through a kilometer of copper,

15
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:55,462
and how the far end pulls
it back out of the noise

16
00:00:55,462 --> 00:00:58,051
— with a good dose of
history along the way.

17
00:00:58,471 --> 00:01:01,682
And as a reference point,
we'll use a network that many

18
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:05,127
engineers already know from
vehicles and machines: CAN bus.

19
00:01:09,782 --> 00:01:13,622
Process instruments have spoken the
same basic language for decades.

20
00:01:14,042 --> 00:01:17,614
The 4–20 mA loop is
brilliantly simple: two wires,

21
00:01:17,614 --> 00:01:22,042
one analog value, and the same
two wires power the instrument.

22
00:01:22,462 --> 00:01:25,412
Four milliamps, not zero,
means zero percent

23
00:01:25,412 --> 00:01:28,094
— so a broken wire is instantly obvious.

24
00:01:29,149 --> 00:01:34,467
In the 1980s, HART added digital
communication by superimposing a small

25
00:01:34,467 --> 00:01:39,560
tone on that current: 1,200 hertz
for a one, 2,200 hertz for a zero.

26
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:44,339
Because the tone averages to zero,
the analog value is untouched

27
00:01:44,339 --> 00:01:47,745
— but the data rate is
only 1,200 bits per second.

28
00:01:48,799 --> 00:01:55,436
In the 1990s, fieldbuses such as FOUNDATION
Fieldbus H1 and PROFIBUS PA went fully

29
00:01:55,436 --> 00:02:01,748
digital, at 31.25 kilobits per second —
shared by every device on the segment.

30
00:02:02,799 --> 00:02:05,663
Meanwhile, the rest of
industry moved to Ethernet,

31
00:02:05,663 --> 00:02:08,068
at a hundred megabits per second and more.

32
00:02:08,488 --> 00:02:11,219
On a logarithmic scale,
the gap is enormous.

33
00:02:12,282 --> 00:02:15,461
So why didn't plain Ethernet
simply run out into the plant?

34
00:02:15,881 --> 00:02:17,396
Four walls stood in the way.

35
00:02:17,749 --> 00:02:18,602
Distance.

36
00:02:19,022 --> 00:02:23,800
Standard 100BASE-TX Ethernet is
limited to one hundred meters per link,

37
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:29,049
while field cables run for hundreds of
meters — sometimes a kilometer or more.

38
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:30,248
Wires.

39
00:02:30,668 --> 00:02:33,106
Office Ethernet needs two or four pairs;

40
00:02:33,106 --> 00:02:35,788
plants are full of
installed two-wire cable.

41
00:02:36,149 --> 00:02:36,896
Power.

42
00:02:37,316 --> 00:02:39,833
A field instrument has no local supply.

43
00:02:40,253 --> 00:02:43,794
It must be powered over the same
two wires that carry its signal.

44
00:02:44,149 --> 00:02:45,621
And explosion protection.

45
00:02:46,041 --> 00:02:49,604
In hazardous areas, the energy
in a circuit must be limited,

46
00:02:49,604 --> 00:02:52,633
so that not even a fault
can ignite the atmosphere.

47
00:02:53,053 --> 00:02:55,997
Ordinary Ethernet was
never designed for that.

48
00:02:56,848 --> 00:03:02,267
Ethernet-APL — the Advanced Physical Layer
— was designed to break all four walls.

49
00:03:02,687 --> 00:03:04,223
The key word is “physical”.

50
00:03:07,882 --> 00:03:11,571
APL replaces only the bottom
layer of the network stack:

51
00:03:11,571 --> 00:03:15,391
the copper, the voltages,
the power, and the safety rules.

52
00:03:15,811 --> 00:03:22,028
Everything above it is unchanged Ethernet:
the same frames and MAC addresses,

53
00:03:22,028 --> 00:03:26,227
IP, TCP and UDP, and the
industrial protocols on top

54
00:03:26,227 --> 00:03:30,425
— PROFINET, EtherNet/IP,
HART-IP, OPC UA, Modbus TCP

55
00:03:30,425 --> 00:03:33,816
— even a web server inside the instrument.

56
00:03:34,782 --> 00:03:41,469
Its foundation is 10BASE-T1L, part of
the IEEE 802.3cg standard from 2019: ten

57
00:03:41,469 --> 00:03:48,670
megabits per second, full duplex, over a
single twisted pair, for up to a kilometer.

58
00:03:49,090 --> 00:03:54,757
On top of that, the APL project — a
joint effort of FieldComm Group, ODVA,

59
00:03:54,757 --> 00:03:59,276
the OPC Foundation, and PROFIBUS
and PROFINET International

60
00:03:59,276 --> 00:04:01,803
— added what process plants need:

61
00:04:01,803 --> 00:04:07,011
port profiles that define how power
is delivered over the same pair,

62
00:04:07,011 --> 00:04:09,232
standardized as IEC TS 63444,

63
00:04:09,232 --> 00:04:15,052
and an intrinsic-safety concept called
2-WISE, published as IEC TS 60079-47.

64
00:04:16,015 --> 00:04:20,356
The result: two wires, ten megabits
per second in both directions,

65
00:04:20,356 --> 00:04:21,934
power and data together,

66
00:04:21,934 --> 00:04:23,710
trunks up to one kilometer,

67
00:04:23,710 --> 00:04:27,919
and intrinsically safe spurs that
reach all the way into Zone 0.

68
00:04:32,582 --> 00:04:35,654
Before we open up APL,
let's set up our reference.

69
00:04:36,074 --> 00:04:38,528
CAN, the Controller Area Network,

70
00:04:38,528 --> 00:04:44,180
was presented by Bosch in 1986 to tame
the growing tangle of wiring in cars.

71
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:48,974
Today it's everywhere: vehicles,
tractors, robots, medical devices.

72
00:04:49,932 --> 00:04:52,124
Physically, CAN is one twisted pair,

73
00:04:52,124 --> 00:04:55,350
called CAN high and CAN low,
running past every node.

74
00:04:55,770 --> 00:04:59,277
A 120-ohm resistor at each
end matches the cable's

75
00:04:59,277 --> 00:05:02,853
characteristic impedance,
so signals don't reflect.

76
00:05:03,815 --> 00:05:05,223
CAN has two bus states.

77
00:05:05,643 --> 00:05:10,020
In the recessive state, nobody drives
the bus: the termination resistors

78
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:14,518
pull both wires to about two and a
half volts, and the difference is zero.

79
00:05:14,938 --> 00:05:19,810
To send a dominant bit, a transceiver
pushes CAN high up to about three and a half

80
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:24,623
volts and pulls CAN low down to one and
a half — a difference of about two volts.

81
00:05:24,982 --> 00:05:27,051
The receiver looks only at the difference.

82
00:05:27,471 --> 00:05:32,093
It's exactly like a differential-pressure
transmitter, which ignores the static

83
00:05:32,093 --> 00:05:37,007
line pressure: interference that couples
equally into both wires simply cancels out.

84
00:05:37,427 --> 00:05:40,499
APL, as we'll see,
uses the very same trick.

85
00:05:41,965 --> 00:05:43,437
Now the crucial property.

86
00:05:43,857 --> 00:05:45,414
Dominant always wins.

87
00:05:45,834 --> 00:05:49,618
If even one node drives a dominant
zero, the bus is dominant,

88
00:05:49,618 --> 00:05:52,533
no matter how many others
send a recessive one.

89
00:05:52,953 --> 00:05:57,646
Engineers call this a wired-AND, and CAN
builds its entire access method on it.

90
00:05:58,598 --> 00:06:01,670
Suppose three nodes start a
frame at the same instant.

91
00:06:02,090 --> 00:06:07,125
Each one sends its eleven-bit identifier,
bit by bit, while listening to the bus.

92
00:06:07,545 --> 00:06:11,381
At the fifth bit, the cabin-temperature
node sends a recessive one,

93
00:06:11,381 --> 00:06:13,156
but reads back a dominant zero.

94
00:06:13,576 --> 00:06:16,436
Someone with a more
important message is talking,

95
00:06:16,436 --> 00:06:18,888
so it stops immediately, and just listens.

96
00:06:19,308 --> 00:06:22,934
Three bits later, the engine-speed
node loses the same way.

97
00:06:23,354 --> 00:06:26,764
The brake-pressure message,
with the lowest identifier,

98
00:06:26,764 --> 00:06:29,306
wins — and its frame continues undamaged.

99
00:06:29,726 --> 00:06:32,990
No collision,
no retransmission, no time lost.

100
00:06:33,410 --> 00:06:37,634
This is bitwise arbitration,
and it's why CAN is so deterministic:

101
00:06:37,634 --> 00:06:41,026
the most important message
always gets through first.

102
00:06:41,982 --> 00:06:45,541
A classic CAN frame carries
at most eight bytes of data,

103
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:48,147
wrapped in nearly fifty bits of overhead:

104
00:06:48,147 --> 00:06:51,198
the identifier, control
bits, a fifteen-bit CRC,

105
00:06:51,198 --> 00:06:54,248
an acknowledge slot,
and an end-of-frame marker.

106
00:06:54,615 --> 00:06:59,226
After five identical bits, the transmitter
inserts one opposite stuff bit,

107
00:06:59,226 --> 00:07:01,719
so the receivers never lose their clock.

108
00:07:02,682 --> 00:07:05,732
Notice what's missing:
there's no destination address.

109
00:07:06,152 --> 00:07:09,554
The identifier names the
content, like “engine speed”,

110
00:07:09,554 --> 00:07:12,766
and every node decides for
itself whether it cares.

111
00:07:14,132 --> 00:07:17,353
But arbitration has a price,
and it is paid in meters.

112
00:07:17,773 --> 00:07:19,283
For the wired-AND to work,

113
00:07:19,283 --> 00:07:23,001
a dominant bit from the far end
of the bus must reach every node

114
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:27,416
— and the transmitter must read back
the result — before the bit is sampled.

115
00:07:27,782 --> 00:07:30,619
Signals travel about five
nanoseconds per meter.

116
00:07:31,039 --> 00:07:33,517
So the round trip to the far end and back,

117
00:07:33,517 --> 00:07:37,588
plus the transceiver delays, has to
fit inside a fraction of one bit.

118
00:07:38,008 --> 00:07:41,898
At one megabit per second,
a bit lasts just one microsecond,

119
00:07:41,898 --> 00:07:44,686
which limits the bus
to about forty meters.

120
00:07:45,048 --> 00:07:46,478
Want two hundred meters?

121
00:07:46,898 --> 00:07:49,820
Then slow down, so that
every bit lasts long enough.

122
00:07:50,782 --> 00:07:53,491
Plot the usual limits on logarithmic axes.

123
00:07:53,911 --> 00:07:55,255
Five hundred meters?

124
00:07:55,675 --> 00:07:57,979
125 kilobits per second.

125
00:07:58,399 --> 00:07:59,338
A kilometer?

126
00:07:59,758 --> 00:08:00,760
About fifty.

127
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:05,575
Bit rate times length is roughly constant:
CAN trades speed for distance.

128
00:08:05,932 --> 00:08:09,452
That's also why CAN FD only
accelerates the data phase.

129
00:08:09,872 --> 00:08:13,138
Once arbitration is over and
a single node is talking,

130
00:08:13,138 --> 00:08:15,376
the round-trip constraint disappears.

131
00:08:16,932 --> 00:08:20,132
One more detail of CAN's
physical layer will matter later.

132
00:08:20,552 --> 00:08:22,002
There is no clock wire.

133
00:08:22,422 --> 00:08:26,220
So how does a receiver know where
one bit ends and the next begins?

134
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:30,022
Each node divides the bit
time into small time quanta,

135
00:08:30,022 --> 00:08:32,528
counted from its own crystal oscillator.

136
00:08:32,948 --> 00:08:35,026
A bit consists of four segments:

137
00:08:35,026 --> 00:08:38,469
a synchronization segment,
where an edge is expected;

138
00:08:38,469 --> 00:08:43,016
a propagation segment, which absorbs
the round-trip delay we just met;

139
00:08:43,016 --> 00:08:44,510
and two phase segments.

140
00:08:44,930 --> 00:08:48,788
The bus is sampled between the two
phase segments — typically somewhere

141
00:08:48,788 --> 00:08:52,482
between seventy-five and eighty-seven
and a half percent of the bit.

142
00:08:53,448 --> 00:08:56,688
Every frame begins with a
hard synchronization on the

143
00:08:56,688 --> 00:09:00,232
very first recessive-to-dominant
edge: the start of frame.

144
00:09:00,652 --> 00:09:05,591
After that, whenever a recessive-to-dominant
edge arrives a little early or late,

145
00:09:05,591 --> 00:09:08,091
the receiver stretches phase segment one,

146
00:09:08,091 --> 00:09:11,810
or shortens phase segment two,
by a few quanta, and realigns.

147
00:09:12,230 --> 00:09:16,417
And that's the real reason for bit
stuffing: after five identical bits

148
00:09:16,417 --> 00:09:20,784
there must be an edge, so the oscillators
can never drift apart for long.

149
00:09:21,204 --> 00:09:22,719
Keep this picture in mind.

150
00:09:23,139 --> 00:09:25,806
CAN recovers its timing
from occasional edges.

151
00:09:26,226 --> 00:09:28,444
APL will need something far finer.

152
00:09:32,598 --> 00:09:36,801
Ethernet itself started out as a
party line, not so different from CAN.

153
00:09:37,221 --> 00:09:41,461
In 1973, at Xerox PARC,
Bob Metcalfe sketched a network in

154
00:09:41,461 --> 00:09:46,650
which every computer tapped into one
shared coaxial cable: the 'ether'.

155
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:49,140
Before sending, a station listened.

156
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:54,141
If two started at the same moment,
their signals collided, and both backed off

157
00:09:54,141 --> 00:09:58,840
and tried again later: carrier sense,
multiple access, with collision detection.

158
00:09:59,798 --> 00:10:02,721
And it carried the very same
physical constraint as CAN.

159
00:10:03,141 --> 00:10:05,860
A sender must still be
transmitting when the news of

160
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:08,474
a collision returns from
the far end of the cable.

161
00:10:08,894 --> 00:10:14,360
That's why classic Ethernet frames have
a minimum length of sixty-four bytes:

162
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:18,406
512 bits, or 51.2 microseconds
at ten megabits per second

163
00:10:18,406 --> 00:10:21,600
— enough to cover the
round trip of a network

164
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,724
a few kilometers across,
repeaters included.

165
00:10:25,144 --> 00:10:27,943
CAN pays for the round
trip in every single bit;

166
00:10:27,943 --> 00:10:30,392
early Ethernet paid for it once per frame.

167
00:10:31,348 --> 00:10:32,799
Then Ethernet escaped.

168
00:10:33,219 --> 00:10:38,083
Twisted-pair cabling arrived in 1990,
first with hubs, then with switches.

169
00:10:38,503 --> 00:10:43,182
Each cable now connected exactly
two devices, and with full duplex,

170
00:10:43,182 --> 00:10:47,442
standardized in the late 1990s,
collisions became impossible.

171
00:10:47,862 --> 00:10:51,424
With nothing to detect,
the round-trip constraint simply vanished.

172
00:10:51,844 --> 00:10:55,629
From then on, the length of a
link is limited by one thing only:

173
00:10:55,629 --> 00:10:57,818
how well a signal survives the cable.

174
00:10:58,782 --> 00:11:02,686
That turned Ethernet into a story
of ever cleverer physical layers.

175
00:11:03,106 --> 00:11:06,007
Manchester code over two pairs in 1990.

176
00:11:06,427 --> 00:11:10,950
Three-level MLT-3 for a
hundred megabits in 1995.

177
00:11:11,370 --> 00:11:17,044
Five levels, echo cancellation and four
pairs for gigabit Ethernet in 1999.

178
00:11:17,464 --> 00:11:20,067
In 2015, a single pair for cars.

179
00:11:20,487 --> 00:11:24,190
And in 2019, 10BASE-T1L
applied the whole toolbox

180
00:11:24,190 --> 00:11:27,591
to a different goal:
not speed, but distance.

181
00:11:29,048 --> 00:11:32,270
Now let's look at Ethernet-APL
with the same eyes.

182
00:11:32,690 --> 00:11:33,991
First, topology.

183
00:11:34,411 --> 00:11:35,798
There is no shared bus.

184
00:11:36,218 --> 00:11:38,506
Every link connects exactly two ports,

185
00:11:38,506 --> 00:11:41,636
point to point, and switches
tie the links together.

186
00:11:42,598 --> 00:11:45,414
A typical plant uses a
trunk-and-spur layout.

187
00:11:45,834 --> 00:11:48,957
In the safe area,
an APL power switch connects

188
00:11:48,957 --> 00:11:52,148
to the plant network and
feeds a trunk cable up

189
00:11:52,148 --> 00:11:55,678
to one kilometer long,
carrying both data and power,

190
00:11:55,678 --> 00:11:58,122
with a 2.4-volt peak-to-peak signal.

191
00:11:58,482 --> 00:12:01,953
Out in the hazardous area,
a field switch takes that power

192
00:12:01,953 --> 00:12:05,543
and distributes it over spurs
up to two hundred meters long.

193
00:12:05,963 --> 00:12:08,453
Each spur serves exactly one instrument,

194
00:12:08,453 --> 00:12:12,064
with a lower one-volt signal
and intrinsically safe power.

195
00:12:12,415 --> 00:12:16,092
And because every spur is its
own link, every instrument gets

196
00:12:16,092 --> 00:12:20,010
its own ten megabits per second,
in each direction, all the time.

197
00:12:20,430 --> 00:12:23,160
Nothing is shared,
so there's nothing to arbitrate.

198
00:12:24,915 --> 00:12:28,328
From here on, we'll follow a single
bit through the physical layer.

199
00:12:28,748 --> 00:12:31,031
A transmitter turns bits into voltages.

200
00:12:31,451 --> 00:12:35,398
A kilometer of twisted pair carries
them — and mangles them on the way.

201
00:12:35,818 --> 00:12:38,932
And a receiver turns whatever
arrives back into bits.

202
00:12:39,352 --> 00:12:41,763
We'll start in the middle,
with the wire itself.

203
00:12:45,415 --> 00:12:49,375
In the summer of 1858,
the first telegraph cable across the

204
00:12:49,375 --> 00:12:53,202
Atlantic went into service,
from Ireland to Newfoundland.

205
00:12:53,622 --> 00:12:55,755
It was a triumph — and a disappointment.

206
00:12:56,175 --> 00:13:00,442
A crisp pulse sent at one end arrived
at the other smeared out over seconds.

207
00:13:00,862 --> 00:13:05,384
Queen Victoria's ninety-eight-word
greeting took most of a day to get through.

208
00:13:06,448 --> 00:13:11,014
A few years earlier, William Thomson —
later Lord Kelvin — had worked out why.

209
00:13:11,434 --> 00:13:15,274
He modelled the cable as a resistance
and a capacitance per meter.

210
00:13:15,694 --> 00:13:18,378
The copper core,
its gutta-percha insulation

211
00:13:18,378 --> 00:13:21,428
and the sea around it form
one enormous capacitor,

212
00:13:21,428 --> 00:13:24,173
and every piece of it
must be charged through

213
00:13:24,173 --> 00:13:26,552
the resistance of the copper before it.

214
00:13:26,972 --> 00:13:30,919
What comes out is exactly the
equation Fourier had written for heat.

215
00:13:32,182 --> 00:13:34,912
So a voltage step doesn't
run down such a cable.

216
00:13:35,332 --> 00:13:39,586
It soaks in, the way heat creeps along
a metal bar that's held in a flame

217
00:13:39,586 --> 00:13:43,780
at one end: quickly near the source,
and ever more slowly further along.

218
00:13:44,732 --> 00:13:48,209
At the far end, a short pulse
shows up as a slow, low hump.

219
00:13:48,629 --> 00:13:52,448
Its peak arrives after a time
proportional to R C x squared.

220
00:13:52,868 --> 00:13:56,205
Double the length, and the
signal takes four times as long,

221
00:13:56,205 --> 00:13:57,902
and arrives four times weaker.

222
00:13:58,322 --> 00:13:59,922
That's Kelvin's law of squares.

223
00:14:00,882 --> 00:14:03,649
The cable's chief electrician,
Wildman Whitehouse,

224
00:14:03,649 --> 00:14:06,471
dismissed the theory as 'a
fiction of the schools'.

225
00:14:06,891 --> 00:14:08,969
He tried to force the signals through

226
00:14:08,969 --> 00:14:11,776
with induction coils of
around two thousand volts.

227
00:14:12,196 --> 00:14:16,058
Within weeks the insulation broke
down, and the cable fell silent.

228
00:14:17,015 --> 00:14:20,671
The cable of 1866 was far
better built, and it worked —

229
00:14:20,671 --> 00:14:25,591
read with Kelvin's mirror galvanometer,
which needs only a minute current.

230
00:14:26,011 --> 00:14:29,864
A tiny current twists a tiny mirror,
and a beam of light turns

231
00:14:29,864 --> 00:14:33,158
that twist into a big swing
of a spot across a scale.

232
00:14:34,115 --> 00:14:37,075
The full picture came
from Oliver Heaviside,

233
00:14:37,075 --> 00:14:40,238
a self-taught telegraph
engineer, in the 1880s.

234
00:14:40,658 --> 00:14:43,607
He added what Kelvin had
left out: the inductance —

235
00:14:43,607 --> 00:14:48,060
the magnetic inertia of the current —
and the leakage through the insulation.

236
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:52,278
His telegrapher's equations
describe one short slice of line.

237
00:14:52,698 --> 00:14:56,730
At low frequencies, resistance wins
and you get Kelvin's diffusion.

238
00:14:57,150 --> 00:15:03,001
But once omega L is much larger than R,
the equations turn into the wave equation

239
00:15:03,001 --> 00:15:07,624
— the same one d'Alembert solved
for a vibrating string in 1747.

240
00:15:08,044 --> 00:15:11,152
Its solutions are shapes
that travel, unchanged,

241
00:15:11,152 --> 00:15:15,426
in both directions, at a speed of
one over the square root of L C.

242
00:15:16,382 --> 00:15:21,672
Side by side: in a resistance-capacitance
line, a pulse spreads out and sinks away.

243
00:15:22,092 --> 00:15:26,999
In an inductance-capacitance line, the same
pulse simply travels, keeping its shape.

244
00:15:27,965 --> 00:15:31,284
For APL's fieldbus cable,
resistance and inductive

245
00:15:31,284 --> 00:15:34,536
reactance are equal at
around thirteen kilohertz.

246
00:15:34,956 --> 00:15:37,473
Below that lies Kelvin's
world of diffusion.

247
00:15:37,893 --> 00:15:39,769
That's where HART's tones live —

248
00:15:39,769 --> 00:15:44,165
which is why HART's cable rules are
written as a maximum R C time constant.

249
00:15:44,585 --> 00:15:46,889
Above it lies Heaviside's world of waves.

250
00:15:47,309 --> 00:15:50,605
APL's signals sit between
about a hundred kilohertz

251
00:15:50,605 --> 00:15:53,837
and a few megahertz:
firmly in the world of waves.

252
00:15:54,257 --> 00:15:55,836
So let's look at those waves.

253
00:15:57,198 --> 00:16:02,041
If the wave equation feels abstract,
picture a chain of masses joined by springs.

254
00:16:02,461 --> 00:16:06,471
Give the first mass a shove, and a
compression pulse runs down the chain.

255
00:16:06,891 --> 00:16:09,579
A transmission line is
the very same system.

256
00:16:09,999 --> 00:16:14,351
Inductance plays the role of mass:
it resists any change in current,

257
00:16:14,351 --> 00:16:16,847
as mass resists any change in velocity.

258
00:16:17,267 --> 00:16:19,817
Capacitance plays the role of the springs:

259
00:16:19,817 --> 00:16:22,366
it stores energy as the voltage builds up.

260
00:16:22,786 --> 00:16:25,879
Voltage is the force;
current is the velocity.

261
00:16:27,398 --> 00:16:31,488
The wave speed follows directly:
one over the square root of L C —

262
00:16:31,488 --> 00:16:36,507
just as a string's wave speed is the square
root of tension over mass per length.

263
00:16:36,927 --> 00:16:40,047
For APL's fieldbus cable,
about half a microhenry

264
00:16:40,047 --> 00:16:43,039
and fifty picofarads per
meter give two hundred

265
00:16:43,039 --> 00:16:45,013
thousand kilometers per second:

266
00:16:45,013 --> 00:16:49,087
two-thirds of the speed of light,
or five nanoseconds per meter.

267
00:16:50,048 --> 00:16:53,269
So a kilometer of cable
is five microseconds long.

268
00:16:53,689 --> 00:16:56,837
That's thirty-seven and a
half APL symbols in flight,

269
00:16:56,837 --> 00:16:58,916
in each direction, at every moment.

270
00:16:59,336 --> 00:17:02,472
The cable isn't a wire that
connects two points instantly.

271
00:17:02,892 --> 00:17:04,151
It's a conveyor belt.

272
00:17:06,315 --> 00:17:10,281
A travelling wave carries voltage
and current in a fixed ratio:

273
00:17:10,281 --> 00:17:12,736
the characteristic impedance, Z-naught.

274
00:17:13,156 --> 00:17:14,073
Here's why.

275
00:17:14,493 --> 00:17:18,032
As the edge advances,
every second it charges another

276
00:17:18,032 --> 00:17:21,171
c meters of cable
capacitance to the voltage V.

277
00:17:21,591 --> 00:17:24,065
That takes a current I equals C c V.

278
00:17:24,485 --> 00:17:29,499
So V over I is one over C c, which works
out to the square root of L over C.

279
00:17:29,919 --> 00:17:31,860
For our cable: a hundred ohms.

280
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:34,136
You can't measure it with an ohmmeter.

281
00:17:34,556 --> 00:17:36,317
It's the load a fast edge sees,

282
00:17:36,317 --> 00:17:40,124
because the edge only knows about
the cable it has already charged.

283
00:17:41,081 --> 00:17:44,815
If you've ever heard water hammer
in a pipe, you've met the same idea.

284
00:17:45,235 --> 00:17:50,401
Slam a valve shut, and the pressure jumps
by rho times a times delta v — Joukowsky's

285
00:17:50,401 --> 00:17:55,197
equation — and that pressure wave runs
back up the pipe at the speed of sound.

286
00:17:55,617 --> 00:17:58,049
It's exactly V equals Z-naught times I.

287
00:17:59,015 --> 00:18:01,724
What happens when the wave
reaches the end of the line?

288
00:18:02,144 --> 00:18:03,488
The mismatch decides.

289
00:18:03,908 --> 00:18:08,094
The reflection coefficient is
gamma equals Z L minus Z-naught,

290
00:18:08,094 --> 00:18:09,647
over Z L plus Z-naught.

291
00:18:10,067 --> 00:18:13,456
At an open end, no current
can flow, so the wave reflects

292
00:18:13,456 --> 00:18:16,488
with the same sign — like
a string with a free end.

293
00:18:16,908 --> 00:18:19,619
At a short circuit,
the voltage must be zero,

294
00:18:19,619 --> 00:18:23,415
so the wave comes back inverted
— like a string tied to a wall.

295
00:18:23,835 --> 00:18:27,453
Terminate the line with a resistor
exactly equal to Z-naught,

296
00:18:27,453 --> 00:18:29,232
and nothing comes back at all.

297
00:18:29,652 --> 00:18:33,343
The resistor swallows the wave
as if the cable went on forever.

298
00:18:33,763 --> 00:18:37,133
Mechanically, it's a dashpot
tuned to the chain's impedance.

299
00:18:38,098 --> 00:18:42,450
That's what CAN's 120-ohm terminators
do at both ends of its bus.

300
00:18:42,870 --> 00:18:45,504
In APL, every link has exactly two ends,

301
00:18:45,504 --> 00:18:49,718
and each transceiver terminates
its own end with a hundred ohms.

302
00:18:50,138 --> 00:18:52,250
Still, no real cable is perfect.

303
00:18:52,670 --> 00:18:55,763
Connectors, splices and
manufacturing tolerances

304
00:18:55,763 --> 00:18:58,985
each reflect a small echo
back towards the sender.

305
00:18:59,405 --> 00:19:02,690
Remember those echoes — the receiver
will have to deal with them.

306
00:19:04,398 --> 00:19:06,958
So far, our waves have
travelled without loss.

307
00:19:07,378 --> 00:19:09,703
Real cables lose energy in two ways.

308
00:19:10,123 --> 00:19:14,240
Current flowing through the copper heats
it: that's the series resistance, R.

309
00:19:14,660 --> 00:19:18,619
And the insulation absorbs a
little energy from the alternating

310
00:19:18,619 --> 00:19:22,767
electric field between the wires:
that's the shunt conductance, G.

311
00:19:23,187 --> 00:19:28,203
On a low-loss line, every meter takes
away the same fraction of the wave,

312
00:19:28,203 --> 00:19:30,745
so the amplitude decays exponentially

313
00:19:30,745 --> 00:19:35,073
— e to the minus alpha x, with
alpha about R over two Z-naught,

314
00:19:35,073 --> 00:19:37,203
plus G times Z-naught over two.

315
00:19:38,164 --> 00:19:40,340
But R is not the DC resistance.

316
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:43,467
At high frequencies,
the current crowds towards

317
00:19:43,467 --> 00:19:46,115
the surface of the conductor:
the skin effect.

318
00:19:46,535 --> 00:19:49,756
You know the same phenomenon from
the ground beneath your feet.

319
00:19:50,176 --> 00:19:54,444
The daily temperature swing at the
surface fades within a few tens of

320
00:19:54,444 --> 00:19:58,774
centimeters of soil; only the slow
seasonal swing reaches meters deep.

321
00:19:59,194 --> 00:20:01,839
Fast changes only penetrate a thin layer.

322
00:20:02,259 --> 00:20:06,054
For the current in a wire,
it's the same diffusion equation,

323
00:20:06,054 --> 00:20:11,304
and the penetration depth is delta equals
the square root of two rho over omega mu.

324
00:20:11,724 --> 00:20:16,951
For copper at 3.75 megahertz,
delta is just thirty-four micrometers.

325
00:20:17,371 --> 00:20:22,371
An 18-gauge conductor is a millimeter
thick, so most of the copper sits idle:

326
00:20:22,371 --> 00:20:25,618
the resistance is almost
eight times its DC value,

327
00:20:25,618 --> 00:20:29,190
and it keeps growing with
the square root of frequency.

328
00:20:30,148 --> 00:20:34,097
Add the dielectric losses, which
grow in proportion to frequency,

329
00:20:34,097 --> 00:20:38,958
and you get the classic cable-loss curve:
a square-root term plus a linear term.

330
00:20:39,378 --> 00:20:45,163
The standard allows up to about
25.6 dB of loss at 3.75 megahertz,

331
00:20:45,163 --> 00:20:47,442
over a one-kilometer link.

332
00:20:47,862 --> 00:20:50,273
In amplitude, that's a factor of nineteen.

333
00:20:50,693 --> 00:20:52,720
And the loss keeps
climbing with frequency.

334
00:20:53,140 --> 00:20:56,681
Low frequencies get through;
high frequencies are stripped away.

335
00:20:57,101 --> 00:21:00,258
The cable is a low-pass
filter, one kilometer long.

336
00:21:01,214 --> 00:21:05,140
Now send a single, crisp symbol
into a kilometer of this cable.

337
00:21:05,560 --> 00:21:08,194
What comes out is lower,
rounder and later —

338
00:21:08,194 --> 00:21:12,685
and it drags a long tail behind it that
lasts for dozens of symbol periods.

339
00:21:13,105 --> 00:21:14,447
Send a stream of symbols,

340
00:21:14,447 --> 00:21:18,204
and every symbol's tail spills into
the decisions about its neighbors.

341
00:21:18,624 --> 00:21:20,309
That's intersymbol interference.

342
00:21:20,729 --> 00:21:25,614
Mathematically, the received signal is
a convolution: each symbol's response,

343
00:21:25,614 --> 00:21:30,436
shifted by one symbol period and scaled
by the symbol's value, all added up.

344
00:21:31,398 --> 00:21:36,155
Overlay every symbol period of the received
waveform, and you get an eye diagram.

345
00:21:36,575 --> 00:21:38,452
Sent, it shows three clean levels.

346
00:21:38,872 --> 00:21:43,647
After a kilometer of cable, the eye
is closed: from the raw voltage alone,

347
00:21:43,647 --> 00:21:46,808
plus, zero and minus can
no longer be told apart.

348
00:21:47,228 --> 00:21:50,258
Prying that eye open again
is the receiver's job.

349
00:21:50,678 --> 00:21:51,574
We'll get there.

350
00:21:52,531 --> 00:21:55,390
One more enemy comes from
outside: interference.

351
00:21:55,810 --> 00:21:59,650
A motor cable nearby produces
a changing magnetic field.

352
00:22:00,070 --> 00:22:04,010
If our two wires ran side by side,
they would form one long loop,

353
00:22:04,010 --> 00:22:08,496
and by Faraday's law that changing
field would induce a voltage around it.

354
00:22:08,916 --> 00:22:11,494
Twist the wires,
and the loop becomes a chain

355
00:22:11,494 --> 00:22:14,015
of small loops with
alternating orientation.

356
00:22:14,435 --> 00:22:17,208
The voltages induced in
neighbouring loops cancel.

357
00:22:17,628 --> 00:22:21,223
Whatever is left appears equally
on both wires — common mode

358
00:22:21,223 --> 00:22:24,818
— and the differential receiver
ignores it, just like CAN's.

359
00:22:25,238 --> 00:22:28,374
And the shield around the pair
takes care of electric fields.

360
00:22:29,831 --> 00:22:31,751
We know what the wire does to a signal.

361
00:22:32,171 --> 00:22:35,734
Now, on to the transmitter:
how should bits become voltages?

362
00:22:39,298 --> 00:22:42,050
Given a cable like this,
how fast can we signal?

363
00:22:42,470 --> 00:22:44,919
Engineers at Bell Labs asked exactly

364
00:22:44,919 --> 00:22:48,592
that about telegraph and
telephone lines in the 1920s.

365
00:22:49,012 --> 00:22:53,336
In 1928, Harry Nyquist showed
that a channel with a bandwidth of

366
00:22:53,336 --> 00:22:57,524
B hertz can carry at most two B
independent pulses per second.

367
00:22:57,944 --> 00:23:00,142
The ideal pulse is the sinc function.

368
00:23:00,562 --> 00:23:03,433
It rings — but its ringing
passes through zero

369
00:23:03,433 --> 00:23:06,428
at exactly every other
pulse's sampling instant.

370
00:23:06,848 --> 00:23:10,484
Line them up, one symbol apart,
sample at the right moments,

371
00:23:10,484 --> 00:23:12,907
and each sample sees only its own pulse.

372
00:23:13,327 --> 00:23:17,188
No interference, in a channel only
half as wide as the symbol rate.

373
00:23:17,608 --> 00:23:24,776
For APL, 7.5 million symbols per second
need at least 3.75 megahertz of bandwidth.

374
00:23:25,196 --> 00:23:27,586
That's the Nyquist
frequency we keep meeting.

375
00:23:28,548 --> 00:23:33,028
The same year, Ralph Hartley, also at
Bell Labs, added the second dimension.

376
00:23:33,448 --> 00:23:36,758
If each pulse can take one
of M distinguishable levels,

377
00:23:36,758 --> 00:23:38,504
it carries log two of M bits.

378
00:23:38,924 --> 00:23:40,353
Two levels: one bit.

379
00:23:40,773 --> 00:23:42,309
Four levels: two bits.

380
00:23:42,729 --> 00:23:44,670
Three levels: about one and a half.

381
00:23:45,090 --> 00:23:47,650
So the bit rate is two B times log two M —

382
00:23:47,650 --> 00:23:51,490
and apparently there's no limit,
if you just use enough levels.

383
00:23:52,448 --> 00:23:56,757
The catch is noise: levels packed too
closely can no longer be told apart.

384
00:23:57,177 --> 00:24:00,804
In 1948, Claude Shannon
settled the question for good.

385
00:24:01,224 --> 00:24:04,220
The capacity of a channel
is C equals B times

386
00:24:04,220 --> 00:24:07,282
log two of one plus the
signal-to-noise ratio.

387
00:24:07,702 --> 00:24:12,396
Below that rate, error-free communication
is possible, with clever enough coding.

388
00:24:12,816 --> 00:24:14,416
Above it, it's impossible.

389
00:24:14,836 --> 00:24:16,542
So the designer has two knobs.

390
00:24:16,962 --> 00:24:20,973
The symbol rate costs bandwidth — and
on our cable, bandwidth means loss.

391
00:24:21,393 --> 00:24:24,252
The number of levels costs
signal-to-noise ratio.

392
00:24:24,672 --> 00:24:27,701
Let's see how 10BASE-T1L
sets those two knobs.

393
00:24:28,664 --> 00:24:31,416
Let's turn those knobs for
ten megabits per second.

394
00:24:31,836 --> 00:24:36,932
The original twisted-pair Ethernet,
10BASE-T, used Manchester code: every bit

395
00:24:36,932 --> 00:24:42,226
is a transition in the middle of its bit
period — up for a one, down for a zero.

396
00:24:42,646 --> 00:24:44,992
It's robust, and it carries its own clock.

397
00:24:45,412 --> 00:24:47,715
But it can need two transitions per bit,

398
00:24:47,715 --> 00:24:50,938
so its energy sits between
about five and ten megahertz.

399
00:24:51,358 --> 00:24:55,198
On a kilometer of cable, that
costs thirty to forty-five dB.

400
00:24:55,618 --> 00:24:56,642
Far too much.

401
00:24:57,598 --> 00:25:00,694
Now compare the options for
ten megabits per second,

402
00:25:00,694 --> 00:25:04,147
by the frequency they need and
the loss at that frequency.

403
00:25:04,567 --> 00:25:09,879
Two levels at ten megabaud: Nyquist at
five megahertz, and thirty dB of loss.

404
00:25:10,299 --> 00:25:17,190
Three levels at 7.5 megabaud:
3.75 megahertz, and 25.6 dB.

405
00:25:17,610 --> 00:25:21,966
Four levels at five megabaud:
2.5 megahertz, about twenty dB —

406
00:25:21,966 --> 00:25:25,198
but now the levels are
packed closer together,

407
00:25:25,198 --> 00:25:29,343
so every dB saved in the cable
is paid for in noise margin.

408
00:25:29,763 --> 00:25:34,246
10BASE-T1L chose three levels:
a compromise between cable loss,

409
00:25:34,246 --> 00:25:40,152
emissions and noise margin — combined with
a code that keeps the signal free of DC.

410
00:25:41,114 --> 00:25:45,850
The code is called 4B3T: four bits
in, three ternary symbols out.

411
00:25:46,270 --> 00:25:50,686
Four bits have sixteen combinations;
three ternary symbols have twenty-seven.

412
00:25:51,106 --> 00:25:54,028
And seven and a half
million symbols per second,

413
00:25:54,028 --> 00:25:58,168
times four-thirds of a bit each,
is exactly ten megabits per second.

414
00:25:59,131 --> 00:26:01,819
The spare combinations
buy something valuable.

415
00:26:02,239 --> 00:26:05,823
Many triplets have a non-zero
sum, like plus, plus, zero.

416
00:26:06,243 --> 00:26:10,930
So each group of four bits gets two
possible codes, mirror images of each other,

417
00:26:10,930 --> 00:26:14,563
and the encoder keeps a running
sum of everything it has sent.

418
00:26:14,983 --> 00:26:17,630
When the sum is positive,
it picks the version

419
00:26:17,630 --> 00:26:19,932
that pulls it back down, and vice versa.

420
00:26:20,352 --> 00:26:23,979
The running sum stays bounded,
so the signal's average is zero.

421
00:26:24,399 --> 00:26:30,715
The idea isn't new: a 4B3T code carried
ISDN over ordinary telephone pairs in

422
00:26:30,715 --> 00:26:37,114
the 1980s — also full duplex, with echo
cancellation, over several kilometers.

423
00:26:38,064 --> 00:26:39,835
Why does DC matter so much?

424
00:26:40,255 --> 00:26:42,346
Because the data path is AC-coupled.

425
00:26:42,766 --> 00:26:45,777
Capacitors and transformers
— and, as we'll see,

426
00:26:45,777 --> 00:26:49,166
the network that lets power
share the pair — block DC.

427
00:26:49,586 --> 00:26:51,740
Electrically, that's a high-pass filter.

428
00:26:52,160 --> 00:26:57,381
Feed it a signal with a DC component,
and the output slowly drifts — baseline wander

429
00:26:57,381 --> 00:27:02,166
— until the levels no longer sit where
the receiver's thresholds expect them.

430
00:27:02,586 --> 00:27:05,039
With a balanced code,
there's nothing to drift.

431
00:27:05,459 --> 00:27:06,952
The baseline stays put.

432
00:27:08,414 --> 00:27:11,081
One step comes before
the coding: scrambling.

433
00:27:11,501 --> 00:27:13,862
Real data is anything but random:

434
00:27:13,862 --> 00:27:17,581
long runs of zeros, idle
patterns, repeated headers.

435
00:27:18,001 --> 00:27:22,966
Sent as they are, they turn into periodic
signals with strong spectral lines —

436
00:27:22,966 --> 00:27:25,767
tones that radiate and
interfere — and long,

437
00:27:25,767 --> 00:27:29,905
monotonous stretches that tell
the receiver nothing about timing.

438
00:27:31,064 --> 00:27:34,321
So the transmitter mixes
every bit with a pseudo-random

439
00:27:34,321 --> 00:27:37,104
sequence from a
linear-feedback shift register:

440
00:27:37,104 --> 00:27:39,058
thirty-three flip-flops in a row,

441
00:27:39,058 --> 00:27:42,136
with two taps fed back
through an exclusive-or gate.

442
00:27:42,556 --> 00:27:46,162
Each data bit is exclusive-or'ed
with the register's output.

443
00:27:46,582 --> 00:27:51,938
The receiver runs an identical register in
step, and applies the same exclusive-or —

444
00:27:51,938 --> 00:27:53,979
which restores the data exactly,

445
00:27:53,979 --> 00:27:58,443
because anything exclusive-or'ed
twice with the same bit is unchanged.

446
00:27:58,798 --> 00:28:03,291
With this feedback polynomial,
the sequence only repeats after two to the

447
00:28:03,291 --> 00:28:08,462
thirty-three, minus one bits: about a quarter
of an hour at ten megabits per second.

448
00:28:08,882 --> 00:28:12,828
The result: whatever the data,
the line signal looks like noise —

449
00:28:12,828 --> 00:28:17,927
a smooth spectrum with no tones, and plenty
of transitions for the receiver's clock.

450
00:28:18,881 --> 00:28:20,268
And one subtle detail.

451
00:28:20,688 --> 00:28:25,748
The two ends of a link use different
polynomials: one plus x to the thirteen
plus x

452
00:28:25,748 --> 00:28:29,223
to the thirty-three for the
master, and its mirror image,

453
00:28:29,223 --> 00:28:31,418
with x to the twenty, for the slave.

454
00:28:31,838 --> 00:28:34,889
That makes the two directions
statistically independent.

455
00:28:35,309 --> 00:28:38,466
Keep that in mind: the echo
canceller will depend on it.

456
00:28:39,431 --> 00:28:41,244
So here's the complete transmitter.

457
00:28:41,664 --> 00:28:44,184
Bits from the Ethernet MAC are scrambled,

458
00:28:44,184 --> 00:28:47,318
grouped in fours, and mapped
onto ternary triplets.

459
00:28:47,738 --> 00:28:52,367
A digital-to-analog converter turns
each symbol into one of three voltages,

460
00:28:52,367 --> 00:28:55,823
and a filter rounds off the
edges to keep emissions low.

461
00:28:56,243 --> 00:28:59,548
Finally, a line driver
with a hundred-ohm source

462
00:28:59,548 --> 00:29:02,508
impedance launches the
wave into the cable:

463
00:29:02,508 --> 00:29:04,917
plus or minus 1.2 volts on a trunk,

464
00:29:04,917 --> 00:29:08,979
or plus or minus half a volt
on an intrinsically safe spur.

465
00:29:10,431 --> 00:29:12,031
Our symbols are on the wire.

466
00:29:12,451 --> 00:29:17,038
But the far end is transmitting too —
at the same time, on the same two wires.

467
00:29:20,598 --> 00:29:22,176
How can that possibly work?

468
00:29:22,596 --> 00:29:24,090
Because the cable is linear.

469
00:29:24,510 --> 00:29:28,584
Two waves travelling in opposite
directions simply pass through each other.

470
00:29:29,004 --> 00:29:34,451
The voltage anywhere on the line is
just their sum: v-plus of x minus c t,

471
00:29:34,451 --> 00:29:38,647
plus v-minus of x plus c t —
d'Alembert's solution again.

472
00:29:39,067 --> 00:29:41,948
On a CAN bus,
two transmitters at once means a

473
00:29:41,948 --> 00:29:44,891
contest between states,
settled by arbitration.

474
00:29:45,311 --> 00:29:47,444
On a linear line, it's just addition.

475
00:29:48,398 --> 00:29:51,448
Telephones have relied on
this for well over a century.

476
00:29:51,868 --> 00:29:56,455
Your voice and the caller's voice share
one pair of wires, and a circuit called a

477
00:29:56,455 --> 00:30:01,212
hybrid separates them, so that you hear the
far end — and only a little of yourself.

478
00:30:01,632 --> 00:30:06,454
Long-distance lines with amplifiers
split the two directions onto four wires.

479
00:30:06,874 --> 00:30:09,868
The hybrids at the ends were
never perfectly balanced,

480
00:30:09,868 --> 00:30:11,642
so a little of your voice leaked

481
00:30:11,642 --> 00:30:13,139
back into the return path —

482
00:30:13,139 --> 00:30:16,410
which is why long-distance
calls used to come with an echo.

483
00:30:17,364 --> 00:30:20,906
Inside an APL transceiver,
the hybrid works like this.

484
00:30:21,326 --> 00:30:24,012
The transmitter drives
the line through a source

485
00:30:24,012 --> 00:30:26,531
resistance equal to the
cable's hundred ohms.

486
00:30:26,951 --> 00:30:30,760
With the line matched, the voltage
at the terminals is half of the

487
00:30:30,760 --> 00:30:34,396
transmitter's voltage — plus
whatever arrives from the far end.

488
00:30:34,816 --> 00:30:38,400
A replica network, built from
the same resistor and a model

489
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:41,984
of the line's impedance,
produces a copy of just that half.

490
00:30:42,404 --> 00:30:47,631
Subtract the two, and ideally your own
signal vanishes, leaving only the far end.

491
00:30:47,998 --> 00:30:53,014
In reality, the cable's impedance isn't
exactly a hundred ohms at every frequency,

492
00:30:53,014 --> 00:30:56,318
and every connector and
splice reflects a little echo.

493
00:30:56,738 --> 00:31:01,303
So the analog hybrid removes a good part
of the echo — but nowhere near enough.

494
00:31:02,264 --> 00:31:03,438
How much is enough?

495
00:31:03,858 --> 00:31:06,951
Our own signal leaves at
2.4 volts peak to peak.

496
00:31:07,371 --> 00:31:13,451
The far end's signal arrives up to 25.6
dB weaker: about an eighth of a volt.

497
00:31:13,871 --> 00:31:17,069
To tell plus, zero and
minus apart reliably,

498
00:31:17,069 --> 00:31:22,447
the receiver needs about 20 dB of
signal-to-noise ratio, plus some margin.

499
00:31:22,867 --> 00:31:27,901
So the leftover echo has to end up around
fifty dB below our own transmission

500
00:31:27,901 --> 00:31:30,974
— a factor of about three
hundred in amplitude.

501
00:31:31,394 --> 00:31:34,210
Across all cables,
lengths and temperatures,

502
00:31:34,210 --> 00:31:37,474
no fixed analog circuit
can manage that on its own.

503
00:31:38,831 --> 00:31:42,372
The rest of the job is done
digitally, by the echo canceller.

504
00:31:42,792 --> 00:31:46,995
Its secret weapon: the transceiver
knows exactly which symbols it has sent.

505
00:31:47,415 --> 00:31:51,328
The echo it receives is those
symbols, convolved with the echo

506
00:31:51,328 --> 00:31:55,240
path's impulse response: first
the leakage through the hybrid,

507
00:31:55,240 --> 00:31:59,658
then the small reflections from
connectors and splices, arriving later

508
00:31:59,658 --> 00:32:04,012
— up to a full round trip,
ten microseconds for a kilometer of cable.

509
00:32:04,964 --> 00:32:09,626
So the canceller is a finite-impulse-response
filter: a delay line that holds

510
00:32:09,626 --> 00:32:14,287
the most recently sent symbols — enough
of them to span the whole round trip.

511
00:32:14,707 --> 00:32:17,992
Each one is multiplied by a weight,
and everything is summed.

512
00:32:18,412 --> 00:32:22,892
That sum is an estimate of the echo,
and it's subtracted from the received signal.

513
00:32:23,312 --> 00:32:27,179
And because the symbols are only
plus one, zero or minus one,

514
00:32:27,179 --> 00:32:32,059
those multiplications come for free:
add the weight, skip it, or subtract it.

515
00:32:33,014 --> 00:32:34,444
How does it find the weights?

516
00:32:34,864 --> 00:32:38,707
It watches the error — whatever
is left after the subtraction

517
00:32:38,707 --> 00:32:40,283
— and after every symbol,

518
00:32:40,283 --> 00:32:43,811
it nudges every weight a little:
weight k changes by mu,

519
00:32:43,811 --> 00:32:47,088
times the error, times the
symbol that sat at tap k.

520
00:32:47,508 --> 00:32:50,186
That's the least-mean-squares algorithm,

521
00:32:50,186 --> 00:32:54,270
published by Bernard Widrow and
Ted Hoff at Stanford in 1960.

522
00:32:54,690 --> 00:32:58,765
It is gradient descent on the squared
error, one tiny step per symbol.

523
00:32:59,185 --> 00:33:03,516
If you know the MIT rule from adaptive
control, you've met its close cousin.

524
00:33:03,936 --> 00:33:06,878
Watch the weights take on
the shape of the echo path,

525
00:33:06,878 --> 00:33:08,821
while the residual echo melts away.

526
00:33:09,241 --> 00:33:12,534
And because it never stops
adapting, the canceller follows

527
00:33:12,534 --> 00:33:15,940
the cable as it warms up in the
sun and cools down at night.

528
00:33:16,898 --> 00:33:18,882
Now the scrambler detail pays off.

529
00:33:19,302 --> 00:33:22,864
The canceller learns by correlating
the error with its own symbols.

530
00:33:23,284 --> 00:33:27,543
If the far end's symbols were correlated
with ours, that second term wouldn't

531
00:33:27,543 --> 00:33:31,967
vanish, and the canceller would start
cancelling part of the far-end signal too.

532
00:33:32,387 --> 00:33:34,765
With different scramblers
in each direction,

533
00:33:34,765 --> 00:33:37,251
the two streams are
statistically independent.

534
00:33:37,671 --> 00:33:40,700
The only thing the canceller
can learn is its own echo.

535
00:33:41,664 --> 00:33:44,728
Adaptive echo cancellers
were developed at Bell

536
00:33:44,728 --> 00:33:47,856
Labs in the 1960s for
satellite telephone calls,

537
00:33:47,856 --> 00:33:50,855
where the trip up to a
geostationary satellite

538
00:33:50,855 --> 00:33:54,635
and back makes echoes return
more than half a second late.

539
00:33:55,055 --> 00:33:58,195
Two decades later, they
put full-duplex digital

540
00:33:58,195 --> 00:34:01,135
data onto ordinary
telephone pairs, in ISDN.

541
00:34:01,555 --> 00:34:06,917
In 1999, gigabit Ethernet ran
four echo-cancelled pairs at once;

542
00:34:06,917 --> 00:34:09,598
in 2015, a single pair for cars.

543
00:34:10,018 --> 00:34:12,919
APL's transceivers are
their direct descendants.

544
00:34:13,881 --> 00:34:17,636
And so we arrive at the deepest difference
between the two physical layers.

545
00:34:18,056 --> 00:34:21,867
CAN uses a nonlinear medium,
where dominant beats recessive,

546
00:34:21,867 --> 00:34:23,709
so that nodes can take turns.

547
00:34:24,129 --> 00:34:29,761
APL uses a linear medium plus signal
processing, so that both ends can talk at
once.

548
00:34:31,414 --> 00:34:35,739
With its own echo removed, the receiver
is left with the far end's signal

549
00:34:35,739 --> 00:34:38,049
— weak, smeared, and with a closed eye.

550
00:34:38,469 --> 00:34:39,664
Time to open it.

551
00:34:43,214 --> 00:34:44,836
Here's the whole receive path.

552
00:34:45,256 --> 00:34:49,864
An amplifier with automatic gain control,
and an analog-to-digital converter.

553
00:34:50,284 --> 00:34:54,814
Then, all in the digital domain:
the echo canceller, the equalizer,

554
00:34:54,814 --> 00:34:59,073
the decision, and finally the
descrambler and the 4B3T decoder.

555
00:34:59,493 --> 00:35:02,904
Two feedback loops complete
the picture: decision feedback,

556
00:35:02,904 --> 00:35:07,472
which we'll meet in a moment, and clock
recovery, which decides when to sample.

557
00:35:08,431 --> 00:35:11,535
Look again at one symbol's
response after the cable,

558
00:35:11,535 --> 00:35:15,236
but now sampled once per symbol,
the way the receiver sees it.

559
00:35:15,656 --> 00:35:18,408
One sample is the largest:
the main cursor.

560
00:35:18,828 --> 00:35:21,667
Before it sits a small
pre-cursor; after it,

561
00:35:21,667 --> 00:35:24,247
a long series of post-cursors: the tail.

562
00:35:24,667 --> 00:35:28,208
Every one of those samples lands
in some other symbol's decision.

563
00:35:29,164 --> 00:35:34,839
A feed-forward equalizer is another FIR
filter, this time on the received samples.

564
00:35:35,259 --> 00:35:39,685
Its weights approximate the inverse of
the cable: it boosts the high frequencies

565
00:35:39,685 --> 00:35:43,558
that the cable stripped away,
so that the two together are flat again.

566
00:35:43,978 --> 00:35:47,903
The catch: boosting high frequencies
also boosts the noise up there.

567
00:35:48,864 --> 00:35:50,998
So the long tail is handled differently.

568
00:35:51,418 --> 00:35:53,874
Look at what one received
sample is made of:

569
00:35:53,874 --> 00:35:58,116
a little of the current symbol, plus
the tails of all the symbols before it.

570
00:35:58,536 --> 00:36:01,735
Here, the tails add up to far
more than the symbol itself

571
00:36:01,735 --> 00:36:03,699
— so a raw decision would be wrong.

572
00:36:04,119 --> 00:36:07,411
But the receiver has already
decided those earlier symbols,

573
00:36:07,411 --> 00:36:09,196
so it knows their tails exactly.

574
00:36:09,616 --> 00:36:12,838
The decision-feedback equalizer
simply subtracts them.

575
00:36:13,258 --> 00:36:15,028
What's left is the current symbol.

576
00:36:15,448 --> 00:36:18,283
In one line: y-n is the filtered sample,

577
00:36:18,283 --> 00:36:21,827
minus the sum of b-j times
the previous decisions.

578
00:36:22,247 --> 00:36:26,300
No noise gets amplified, because
past decisions are clean numbers.

579
00:36:26,720 --> 00:36:29,830
The price: a wrong decision
subtracts the wrong tail,

580
00:36:29,830 --> 00:36:32,587
so one error can drag a
few more along with it.

581
00:36:33,548 --> 00:36:37,961
Adaptive equalizers like these
were pioneered by Robert Lucky

582
00:36:37,961 --> 00:36:42,230
at Bell Labs in 1965, to push
data through telephone lines.

583
00:36:42,650 --> 00:36:45,414
Like the echo canceller,
they adapt continuously,

584
00:36:45,414 --> 00:36:47,557
with the same least-mean-squares rule.

585
00:36:47,977 --> 00:36:49,278
Watch the eye open.

586
00:36:52,681 --> 00:36:56,556
After the equalizer, the decision
itself is beautifully simple:

587
00:36:56,556 --> 00:37:00,062
a slicer with two thresholds,
halfway between the levels.

588
00:37:00,482 --> 00:37:02,573
Above the upper threshold: plus one.

589
00:37:02,993 --> 00:37:05,020
Below the lower one: minus one.

590
00:37:05,440 --> 00:37:06,848
In between: zero.

591
00:37:07,198 --> 00:37:09,736
But noise smears each
level into a bell curve.

592
00:37:10,156 --> 00:37:13,742
An error happens whenever a
sample strays past a threshold:

593
00:37:13,742 --> 00:37:18,178
the area in the tail of the bell curve,
given by the Gaussian Q-function.

594
00:37:18,598 --> 00:37:23,318
For an error rate of one in a billion,
each threshold must sit about six standard

595
00:37:23,318 --> 00:37:28,155
deviations away from its level — so
neighbouring levels must be twelve sigma
apart.

596
00:37:28,575 --> 00:37:32,564
That's a signal-to-noise ratio
of about 20 dB at the slicer.

597
00:37:32,984 --> 00:37:35,907
That number is what the
whole design is built around.

598
00:37:36,327 --> 00:37:39,963
Every dB the cable takes,
every trace of residual echo,

599
00:37:39,963 --> 00:37:43,004
every pickup from a
nearby motor eats into it.

600
00:37:43,424 --> 00:37:47,648
And 10BASE-T1L has no forward
error correction to fall back on:

601
00:37:47,648 --> 00:37:49,995
the margin is the error correction.

602
00:37:50,948 --> 00:37:53,870
One question remains:
when exactly to sample?

603
00:37:54,290 --> 00:37:57,006
There's no clock wire,
and the two ends run on

604
00:37:57,006 --> 00:38:00,136
separate crystal oscillators,
a few millionths apart.

605
00:38:00,556 --> 00:38:05,185
So the receiver recovers the clock from the
signal itself, with a phase-locked loop.

606
00:38:05,605 --> 00:38:08,488
A timing detector compares
samples with decisions,

607
00:38:08,488 --> 00:38:12,005
and estimates whether the receiver
is sampling early or late.

608
00:38:12,425 --> 00:38:15,554
That error drives a
proportional-integral controller,

609
00:38:15,554 --> 00:38:20,276
which steers a numerically controlled
oscillator — the receiver's sense of time.

610
00:38:20,696 --> 00:38:22,573
You know this loop from control theory.

611
00:38:22,993 --> 00:38:25,879
The oscillator integrates
frequency into phase;

612
00:38:25,879 --> 00:38:28,704
the integral term removes
the steady frequency

613
00:38:28,704 --> 00:38:30,423
offset between the crystals;

614
00:38:30,423 --> 00:38:34,108
the proportional term keeps the
loop stable and well damped.

615
00:38:34,528 --> 00:38:36,118
Within a few thousand symbols,

616
00:38:36,118 --> 00:38:39,882
the sampling instant settles in the
middle of the eye, and stays there.

617
00:38:40,498 --> 00:38:41,921
Compare that with CAN,

618
00:38:41,921 --> 00:38:46,450
which nudges its bit timing on
occasional edges, by whole time quanta.

619
00:38:46,870 --> 00:38:51,691
APL's receiver tracks the phase continuously,
to a small fraction of a symbol.

620
00:38:52,648 --> 00:38:54,056
And there's one more twist.

621
00:38:54,476 --> 00:38:57,184
The slave doesn't transmit
on its own crystal:

622
00:38:57,184 --> 00:39:00,364
it sends using the clock it
recovered from the master.

623
00:39:00,784 --> 00:39:05,694
Both directions then tick in lockstep —
which is exactly what the echo canceller

624
00:39:05,694 --> 00:39:10,789
needs, because its own echo and the far
end's symbols now share the same time grid.

625
00:39:12,248 --> 00:39:16,002
All of this has to come alive within
moments of plugging in a cable.

626
00:39:16,422 --> 00:39:21,628
First, the two ends exchange a few slow,
simple messages: auto-negotiation.

627
00:39:22,048 --> 00:39:26,684
Using a robust low-speed code,
they agree on the transmit amplitude —

628
00:39:26,684 --> 00:39:31,925
2.4 or 1 volt peak to peak — and on which
end will be master, and which slave.

629
00:39:32,881 --> 00:39:34,225
Then training begins.

630
00:39:34,645 --> 00:39:39,232
The master sends a scrambled stream of
idle symbols, while the slave stays silent.

631
00:39:39,652 --> 00:39:42,751
The slave's receiver locks
its clock to the master,

632
00:39:42,751 --> 00:39:46,094
trains its equalizer,
and synchronizes its descrambler.

633
00:39:46,514 --> 00:39:50,248
Only then does the slave start
transmitting — on the master's clock.

634
00:39:50,668 --> 00:39:52,970
Now the master trains its own receiver,

635
00:39:52,970 --> 00:39:55,745
and both sides fine-tune
their echo cancellers.

636
00:39:56,165 --> 00:39:59,222
When both ends report that
their receivers are happy,

637
00:39:59,222 --> 00:40:01,818
the link is up,
and Ethernet frames can flow.

638
00:40:03,581 --> 00:40:05,608
Let's follow one bit from end to end.

639
00:40:06,028 --> 00:40:10,294
It's scrambled, grouped with three others,
and turned into three ternary symbols.

640
00:40:10,714 --> 00:40:13,364
A voltage step launches
a wave into the cable,

641
00:40:13,364 --> 00:40:18,145
which carries it at two-thirds of the speed
of light, strips away about ninety-five

642
00:40:18,145 --> 00:40:21,716
percent of its amplitude,
and smears it across its neighbors —

643
00:40:21,716 --> 00:40:25,690
all while a wave travelling the
other way passes straight through it.

644
00:40:26,110 --> 00:40:31,390
At the far end, the receiver subtracts its
own echo, undoes the smearing, picks the

645
00:40:31,390 --> 00:40:36,606
right instant to sample, decides plus,
zero or minus, and decodes and descrambles.

646
00:40:37,026 --> 00:40:39,896
Five microseconds and a
few clever filters later,

647
00:40:39,896 --> 00:40:43,234
the bit is back — with fewer
than one error in a billion.

648
00:40:47,498 --> 00:40:51,530
Power travels on the same two wires,
and the trick is frequency, again.

649
00:40:51,950 --> 00:40:54,552
Power is direct current, at zero hertz.

650
00:40:54,972 --> 00:40:59,464
The data lives between roughly a
hundred kilohertz and a few megahertz

651
00:40:59,464 --> 00:41:03,570
— and, thanks to the DC-free code,
it contains no DC of its own.

652
00:41:04,531 --> 00:41:06,258
So at each end of the cable,

653
00:41:06,258 --> 00:41:10,143
inductors pass the direct current
to and from the power supply,

654
00:41:10,143 --> 00:41:12,980
but block the fast data,
while capacitors pass

655
00:41:12,980 --> 00:41:15,816
the data to the transceiver,
but block the DC.

656
00:41:16,236 --> 00:41:20,554
A mechanical engineer would recognize
it immediately: a static load

657
00:41:20,554 --> 00:41:25,644
with a vibration riding on top, separated
by a low-pass and a high-pass filter.

658
00:41:26,498 --> 00:41:28,183
Some numbers make it concrete.

659
00:41:28,603 --> 00:41:31,612
In a typical design, at 3.75 megahertz,

660
00:41:31,612 --> 00:41:36,549
a 220-microhenry inductor has an
impedance of about five kilohms

661
00:41:36,549 --> 00:41:41,872
— fifty times the line's hundred ohms,
so it barely loads the signal.

662
00:41:42,292 --> 00:41:47,946
A 220-nanofarad capacitor, at the same
frequency, is just a fifth of an ohm.

663
00:41:48,366 --> 00:41:51,011
At DC, it's exactly the other way round.

664
00:41:51,964 --> 00:41:54,375
Why does the trunk run
at up to fifty volts?

665
00:41:54,795 --> 00:41:57,718
Because the cable's
resistance burns I squared R.

666
00:41:58,138 --> 00:42:00,616
Deliver the same power
at twice the voltage,

667
00:42:00,616 --> 00:42:03,770
and the current halves — so
the loss drops to a quarter.

668
00:42:04,190 --> 00:42:05,555
And there's a hard ceiling.

669
00:42:05,975 --> 00:42:10,924
With a source voltage V and a loop
resistance R, the most power that can ever

670
00:42:10,924 --> 00:42:16,194
reach the far end is V squared over four
R — and only at fifty percent efficiency.

671
00:42:16,614 --> 00:42:20,347
So a long, powerful trunk needs
both: voltage, and copper.

672
00:42:21,298 --> 00:42:22,343
How much power?

673
00:42:22,763 --> 00:42:25,389
An APL power switch can
feed a trunk with up

674
00:42:25,389 --> 00:42:27,776
to about ninety-two watts at the source.

675
00:42:28,196 --> 00:42:31,498
An instrument on an intrinsically
safe spur gets roughly

676
00:42:31,498 --> 00:42:34,682
half a watt to one watt,
depending on its power class.

677
00:42:35,102 --> 00:42:39,340
That sounds modest — but it's more
than ten times the forty milliwatts or

678
00:42:39,340 --> 00:42:42,824
so that a four-to-twenty milliamp
instrument has to live on.

679
00:42:47,181 --> 00:42:49,933
Now the fourth wall:
explosive atmospheres.

680
00:42:50,353 --> 00:42:54,812
An explosion needs three things:
fuel, oxygen, and an ignition source.

681
00:42:55,232 --> 00:42:58,432
In a process plant, you can't
always remove the first two.

682
00:42:59,598 --> 00:43:01,582
Areas are classified into zones.

683
00:43:02,002 --> 00:43:07,167
Zone 0: an explosive atmosphere is
present continuously or for long periods

684
00:43:07,167 --> 00:43:09,234
— inside a tank, for instance.

685
00:43:09,654 --> 00:43:12,214
Zone 1: likely in normal operation.

686
00:43:12,634 --> 00:43:16,090
Zone 2: not expected,
and only briefly if it happens.

687
00:43:17,048 --> 00:43:19,821
Intrinsic safety removes
the ignition source.

688
00:43:20,241 --> 00:43:24,663
Voltage, current and power are
limited — and so is the energy stored

689
00:43:24,663 --> 00:43:28,889
in the circuit's capacitance and
inductance, one half C V squared

690
00:43:28,889 --> 00:43:33,311
and one half L I squared — so that
even the spark from a broken wire

691
00:43:33,311 --> 00:43:37,862
or a short circuit stays below the
minimum ignition energy of the gas.

692
00:43:38,282 --> 00:43:41,312
For hydrogen, that's only
about twenty microjoules.

693
00:43:42,364 --> 00:43:44,934
Protection level “i-a” means the circuit

694
00:43:44,934 --> 00:43:47,826
stays safe even with
two simultaneous faults.

695
00:43:48,246 --> 00:43:50,443
That's what's required to enter Zone 0.

696
00:43:51,398 --> 00:43:55,443
Traditionally, every intrinsically
safe loop needed a calculation:

697
00:43:55,443 --> 00:43:57,036
does the source's voltage,

698
00:43:57,036 --> 00:43:59,059
current and power fit the device,

699
00:43:59,059 --> 00:44:02,982
and does the cable's capacitance
and inductance fit what's left?

700
00:44:03,402 --> 00:44:06,559
2-WISE replaces that with
predefined port profiles.

701
00:44:06,979 --> 00:44:11,463
A 2-WISE field-switch port and a 2-WISE
instrument with matching profiles

702
00:44:11,463 --> 00:44:16,131
are designed to be safe together,
on up to two hundred meters of spur cable.

703
00:44:16,551 --> 00:44:20,647
You compare the labels, and you're done
— much like FISCO did for fieldbus.

704
00:44:20,998 --> 00:44:24,083
CAN has no standardized
plug-and-play equivalent:

705
00:44:24,083 --> 00:44:28,742
intrinsically safe CAN systems exist,
but they're engineered case by case.

706
00:44:29,162 --> 00:44:33,002
For APL, intrinsic safety was
a design goal from day one.

707
00:44:37,364 --> 00:44:39,156
Let's put the two side by side.

708
00:44:39,576 --> 00:44:40,451
Bandwidth.

709
00:44:40,871 --> 00:44:43,849
On classic CAN, one megabit
per second at most —

710
00:44:43,849 --> 00:44:47,634
and that's at forty meters —
shared by every node on the bus.

711
00:44:48,054 --> 00:44:50,827
With ten nodes, each gets,
on average, a tenth.

712
00:44:51,247 --> 00:44:56,580
On APL, every device has a private ten
megabits per second, in each direction.

713
00:44:57,531 --> 00:44:58,384
Distance.

714
00:44:58,804 --> 00:45:01,556
Back to our chart of bit
rate versus cable length.

715
00:45:01,976 --> 00:45:05,560
CAN falls along that diagonal:
double the length, halve the speed.

716
00:45:05,980 --> 00:45:11,292
10BASE-T1L stays flat at ten megabits
per second, all the way to a kilometer.

717
00:45:11,712 --> 00:45:14,912
Its speed-distance product is
about two hundred times higher.

718
00:45:15,864 --> 00:45:16,696
Frames.

719
00:45:17,116 --> 00:45:20,466
A CAN frame carries up to eight
bytes, and names its content.

720
00:45:20,886 --> 00:45:24,210
An Ethernet frame carries
up to fifteen hundred bytes,

721
00:45:24,210 --> 00:45:27,843
and names its destination and
its source with MAC addresses

722
00:45:27,843 --> 00:45:32,768
— while IP addresses let it be routed
across the whole plant network, or beyond.

723
00:45:33,731 --> 00:45:35,224
What does that mean in practice?

724
00:45:35,644 --> 00:45:39,676
Take one megabyte — say,
a firmware update, or a diagnostic log.

725
00:45:40,096 --> 00:45:41,952
Over HART: almost two hours.

726
00:45:42,372 --> 00:45:44,548
Over a fieldbus: about four minutes.

727
00:45:44,968 --> 00:45:48,638
Over CAN at one megabit per
second: around fifteen seconds.

728
00:45:49,058 --> 00:45:51,788
Over Ethernet-APL: less than a second.

729
00:45:52,748 --> 00:45:56,780
Power, and hazardous areas:
CAN leaves both to the system designer.

730
00:45:57,200 --> 00:46:02,405
APL delivers power over the data pair,
and it is intrinsically safe by design.

731
00:46:04,364 --> 00:46:08,076
And underneath, the two physical
layers could hardly be more different.

732
00:46:08,496 --> 00:46:12,370
CAN: two levels, bit stuffing,
timing from occasional edges,

733
00:46:12,370 --> 00:46:15,920
a nonlinear wired-AND,
and no signal processing at all.

734
00:46:16,340 --> 00:46:21,344
APL: three levels, scrambled and
4B3T-coded, a phase-locked clock,

735
00:46:21,344 --> 00:46:26,196
a linear line with echo cancellation,
and adaptive equalization.

736
00:46:27,948 --> 00:46:29,718
So is APL simply better?

737
00:46:30,138 --> 00:46:30,800
No.

738
00:46:31,220 --> 00:46:35,702
CAN is cheap: a CAN controller is
built into countless microcontrollers,

739
00:46:35,702 --> 00:46:38,068
and its frames are tiny and efficient.

740
00:46:38,488 --> 00:46:42,700
Its arbitration gives hard,
priority-based timing on short buses

741
00:46:42,700 --> 00:46:45,464
— exactly what vehicles and machines need.

742
00:46:45,814 --> 00:46:48,542
Ethernet-APL solves a different problem:

743
00:46:48,542 --> 00:46:52,021
long cables, hazardous areas,
power over two wires,

744
00:46:52,021 --> 00:46:54,544
and rich data from every instrument —

745
00:46:54,544 --> 00:46:58,977
using the same IP-based networking
as the rest of the enterprise.

746
00:47:00,831 --> 00:47:01,940
So, to sum up.

747
00:47:02,360 --> 00:47:03,619
CAN is a party line.

748
00:47:04,039 --> 00:47:08,275
Everyone shares one pair of wires,
the most important voice always wins,

749
00:47:08,275 --> 00:47:11,335
and speed and distance trade
off against each other.

750
00:47:11,698 --> 00:47:15,372
Ethernet-APL is a private
line from every instrument:

751
00:47:15,372 --> 00:47:19,186
point to point, full duplex,
ten megabits per second up

752
00:47:19,186 --> 00:47:22,860
to a kilometer, power and
data on the same two wires,

753
00:47:22,860 --> 00:47:26,119
and intrinsically safe
all the way into Zone 0.

754
00:47:27,081 --> 00:47:29,727
And underneath that private line sits more

755
00:47:29,727 --> 00:47:32,436
than a century of ideas:
Heaviside's waves,

756
00:47:32,436 --> 00:47:35,776
Nyquist's and Shannon's limits,
the telephone hybrid,

757
00:47:35,776 --> 00:47:39,241
Widrow and Hoff's adaptive
filters, Lucky's equalizers.

758
00:47:39,661 --> 00:47:44,376
All of it packed into a transceiver chip
that runs on well under a tenth of a watt.

759
00:47:45,331 --> 00:47:46,718
The four walls are down.

760
00:47:47,138 --> 00:47:51,954
After decades of four to twenty milliamps,
the process plant can finally speak the

761
00:47:51,954 --> 00:47:54,420
same language as the rest of the network —

762
00:47:54,420 --> 00:47:56,887
from the sensor, all the way to the cloud.

763
00:47:58,898 --> 00:48:00,135
Thanks for watching.
